From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Enrico=20B=C3=BChler?= <enrico.buehler@unom.io>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 14:20:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 24/33] pipewire: keep dmabuf capture buffers in device memory

stream_handle_add_buffer marks every capture texture bMappable, and a
mappable texture is allocated HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT | HOST_CACHED.
On a discrete GPU that memory type is system RAM. Only copy_buffer's
MemFd branch reads the texture through its mapping; the DmaBuf branch
hands out tex->dmabuf() and never touches it.

So on a discrete GPU every capture composite writes the whole frame
across PCIe, and a consumer that imports the dmabuf for a hardware
encoder reads it back across PCIe. The compositor blocks in vulkan_wait
on each one, and whatever is rendering gets the GPU time that is left.
An APU shares one memory, which is why it goes unnoticed there.

Measured on an RTX 5070 Ti, headless 5120x1440 at 240 Hz, vkcube, a
PipeWire consumer feeding NVENC: 23 captured frames a second at 100% GPU
and 9.6 GB/s of PCIe traffic before, 240 a second at 12% and 0.06 GB/s
after. 3840x2160 at 120 Hz goes from 49 to 120.

Ask for the mapping only when the buffer is not a dmabuf. The texture
stays LINEAR and exportable; its stride comes from the subresource
layout, not from the mapping.
---
 src/meson.build  | 3 ++-
 src/pipewire.cpp | 5 ++++-
 2 files changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/meson.build b/src/meson.build
index ff79b78..b1c619c 100644
--- a/src/meson.build
+++ b/src/meson.build
@@ -202,7 +202,8 @@ vcs_tag = run_command(vcs_tag_cmd, check: false).stdout().strip()
 #   +pfhdr17 — …and a capture push never drops a buffer the PipeWire thread has not taken
 #   +pfhdr18 — …and the capture stream is reliable and cycles while it waits for its consumer
 #   +pfhdr19 — …and GAMESCOPE_SET_OUTPUT_MODE changes a headless output's mode at runtime
-version_tag = vcs_tag + '+pfhdr19' + ' (' + compiler_name + ' ' + compiler_version + ')'
+#   +pfhdr20 — …and dmabuf capture buffers live in device memory, not system RAM
+version_tag = vcs_tag + '+pfhdr20' + ' (' + compiler_name + ' ' + compiler_version + ')'
 
 gamescope_version_conf = configuration_data()
 gamescope_version_conf.set('VCS_TAG', version_tag)
diff --git a/src/pipewire.cpp b/src/pipewire.cpp
index f5f2ac3..674faed 100644
--- a/src/pipewire.cpp
+++ b/src/pipewire.cpp
@@ -655,7 +655,10 @@ static void stream_handle_add_buffer(void *user_data, struct pw_buffer *pw_buffe
 
 	buffer->texture = new CVulkanTexture();
 	CVulkanTexture::createFlags screenshotImageFlags;
-	screenshotImageFlags.bMappable = true;
+	// Only the MemFd path reads the texture through its mapping (copy_buffer). A mappable
+	// image is HOST_CACHED memory: system RAM on a discrete GPU, so every capture composite
+	// would cross PCIe and the consumer's import would cross it back.
+	screenshotImageFlags.bMappable = !is_dmabuf;
 	screenshotImageFlags.bTransferDst = true;
 	screenshotImageFlags.bStorage = true;
 	if (is_dmabuf || drmFormat == DRM_FORMAT_NV12 || drmFormat == DRM_FORMAT_P010)
